{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"simpson s paradox simpson s paradox","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md","fileName":"Simpson's Paradox.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Simpson's Paradox","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 20:21\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Batch Effects and Latent Factor Adjustment]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Batch Effects and Latent Factor Adjustment","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"simpson s paradox simpson s paradox","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md","fileName":"Simpson's Paradox.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Simpson's Paradox","heading":"Simpson's Paradox","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Simpson's paradox occurs when an association observed in aggregated data reverses or disappears after the data are stratified by a third variable. Unequal group composition lets the stratifying variable create a misleading overall comparison.\n\nThe source uses university admissions to show that an apparent gender difference was driven by application patterns across departments with different admission rates. The example demonstrates why correlation alone does not establish a direct causal relationship.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Batch Effects and Latent Factor Adjustment","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Simpson's Paradox.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearch